# -*- text -*-
# =============================================================================
# WAVLY WIFI - control attributes
# =============================================================================
# Install as /usr/share/freeradius/dictionary.wavly and add to
# /etc/freeradius/3.0/dictionary:
#     $INCLUDE /usr/share/freeradius/dictionary.wavly
#
# EVERY ATTRIBUTE HERE IS A SERVER-SIDE CONTROL ATTRIBUTE. None of them is ever
# placed in an Access-Accept, so none of them goes on the wire and none of them
# can be seen or forged by a customer. They exist to carry authorization and
# quota state from the SQL module into unlang policy.
#
# The vendor id 52381 is a PLACEHOLDER. It is unassigned by IANA and must be
# replaced with a real Private Enterprise Number before any of these attributes
# is ever sent to a NAS. Today that never happens, so the collision risk is
# nil - but a placeholder that looks like a real PEN would eventually be
# copy-pasted into a reply packet by someone who did not read this comment.
#
# WHY NOT Tmp-Integer-0 / Tmp-String-1?
# That is the common shortcut, and it works, but the result is unlang that reads
#   if (&control:Tmp-Integer-0 > &control:Tmp-String-1)
# with no way to tell which is the cap and which is the usage six months later.
# Naming them costs one dictionary file and makes the FUP policy self-documenting
# at the exact place where getting the comparison backwards means giving away
# unlimited data.
# =============================================================================

VENDOR		Wavly					52381

BEGIN-VENDOR	Wavly

# --- data quota, in BYTES -----------------------------------------------------
# integer64, not integer: a monthly cap of 250 GB is 268435456000, which
# overflows a 32-bit attribute (max 4294967295, i.e. ~4 GiB). Using `integer`
# here would not fail loudly - it would wrap, and a customer who had used 5 GB
# would compare as being under a truncated cap.
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Data-Cap				1	integer64
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Used-Bytes			2	integer64

# --- fair usage ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The Mikrotik-Rate-Limit string to CoA into a live session when the cap is
# crossed, and whether FUP is already applied. Without the second flag every
# interim accounting update would re-send the same CoA - MikroTik would cope, but
# it churns the queue tree and fills the router log.
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Fup-Rate				3	string
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Fup-Active			4	integer

# --- device control -----------------------------------------------------------
# max_registered_devices + is_bound from the cloud `users` row. These are the
# attributes that actually stop one subscription being shared across a village:
# Simultaneous-Use only limits sessions that overlap in time, so two people
# alternating on the same account never trip it.
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Mac-Binding			5	integer
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Max-Devices			6	integer
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Device-Count			7	integer

# --- account state ------------------------------------------------------------
# Carried so a reject can say WHY. "Your plan expired on 3 May" is a message a
# customer can act on; a bare Access-Reject makes them call you.
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-User-Status			8	string
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Expires-At			9	string
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Plan-Code				10	string

# --- derived at request time, never written by syncd ---------------------------
# Wavly-Used-Baseline IS written by syncd (bytes already accounted for before the
# current session, so a reconnect does not reset the cap). The other two are
# scratch space for the policies: a validated MAC and the roster decision.
#
# They need dictionary entries even though nothing persists them, because unlang
# cannot reference an attribute the dictionary does not define.
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Used-Baseline			11	integer64
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Mac				12	string
ATTRIBUTE	Wavly-Device-Decision			13	string

END-VENDOR	Wavly
